NNAAMMEE
       scriptreplay - play back typescripts, using timing information

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy [options] [--tt] _t_i_m_i_n_g_f_i_l_e [_t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t [_d_i_v_i_s_o_r]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program replays a typescript, using timing information to ensure
       that output happens in the same rhythm as it originally appeared when
       the script was recorded.

       The replay simply displays the information again; the programs that
       were run when the typescript was being recorded are nnoott rruunn aaggaaiinn.
       Since the same information is simply being displayed, ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy is
       only guaranteed to work properly if run on the same type of terminal
       the typescript was recorded on. Otherwise, any escape characters in the
       typescript may be interpreted differently by the terminal to which
       ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy is sending its output.

       The timing information is what ssccrriipptt(1) outputs to file specified by
       ----lloogg--ttiimmiinngg.

       By default, the typescript to display is assumed to be named
       _t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t, but other filenames may be specified, as the second
       parameter or with option ----lloogg--oouutt.

       If the third parameter or ----ddiivviissoorr is specified, it is used as a
       speed-up multiplier. For example, a speed-up of 2 makes ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy go
       twice as fast, and a speed-down of 0.1 makes it go ten times slower
       than the original session.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
